  "id": "chart-residuals-against-interval",
  "type": "scatter",
  "title": "Residuals from the fitted line, plotted against the dinner-to-sleep interval",
  "alt": "Scatter of 60 residuals against the dinner-to-sleep interval, spread above and below a horizontal zero line with no visible bend or fanning.",
  "longDescription": "Each dot is one person's residual: their observed fasting glucose minus the glucose the fitted line gives at their interval. The horizontal axis is the same interval in hours as the previous chart; the vertical axis is the residual in mg/dL, and the horizontal line marks a residual of zero, where an observation falls exactly on the fitted line. Residuals run from -47.6 mg/dL for the person at 1.7 hours whose glucose was 100 to +34.1 mg/dL for the person at 4.3 hours whose glucose was 173. They sum to zero, which is a property of the least-squares fit and not evidence about it. Read the plot for structure: the dots sit above and below the zero line across the whole range with no arch, no bowl, and no funnel, so nothing here argues that a straight line is the wrong shape or that the spread changes with the interval. The band is wide everywhere, roughly 17 mg/dL either side of zero, which is what a weak fit looks like when the straight-line form is nevertheless adequate.",
